hey guys so I've had quite a few people  asking me questions about how the  structure of Oxbridge works so how the  the college system and the department  system works within the university so I  thought I'd answer those questions once  and for all in a video  this might sound obvious to some of you  in which case you're perfectly free to  not watch this video so every student  Oxbridge is a member of a college and  also a student a department or in some  cases multiple departments those two  systems though the colleges and the  departments are completely separate your  college is our new home and it provides  you with a place to sleep  place to eat and library to work in a  common room and a bar your department on  the other hand is where academics in  your subject have their offices and  where they conduct their research it's  often where you'll have your subjects  lectures and there'll be a subject  specific library there more often than  not in some subjects there's also  laboratories on site where you conduct  practical work so to give you an example  you could be a student Oxford because  it's the best university in the world  studying physics because it's the best  subject in the world at some piece it's  because it's the best college and well  then your home your College site is here  on you in Hall Street while your  department where you have your lectures  is up here on Parks Road those two  systems of buildings don't talk to one  another they're completely separate  other than being labeled under the  umbrella term of the University of  Oxford where things get a little bit  more complicated is when it comes to  your teachings but Oxford your teaching  is roughly divided between two systems  lectures and tutorials or supervisions  depending on which university our  lectures are probably similar to your  current experience of Education students  taking your subject from all the  colleges in the university all attend  lectures which will either be held in  your department or in some other central  location like the exam scores in Oxford  for example an academic will stand at  the front of the lecture hall and talk  for an hour about a subject and either  they will give out printed notes that  you can annotate all you can take notes  free hand generally you'll have a  different academic for each lecture  course that you take and electric course  can range from being a single lecture to  being dozens of lectures long tutorials  or supervisions on the other hand are  really quite different and then what  make Oxbridge unique because I have an  Oxford I'm gonna refer to them for the  rest this video as tutorials just know  that in Cambridge they're called  supervisions and because I'm a scientist  I'm going to start by explaining how  tutorials work for scientists and then  I'm going to talk about how they work  for Humanity students because they are  slightly different  as a scientist your lecturers will hand  out periodically properties which  contain quest  based on the material you've been  covering in lectures up to that point  often this is weekly you complete these  questions and at a pre specified date  you hand them in to one of your college  tutors these are members of academic  staff who are associated with your  college and any college will typically  have several tutors for each subject  they can be lecturers professors PhD  students and in some cases they will  actually choose in multiple colleges so  students from multiple colleges will be  tutored by the same person sometimes in  the tutorial you will sit down with your  tutor either individually or with a  group of other people from your college  that do your subject typically one or  two others they'll give you back your  script of attempted solutions marks and  they'll go through the questions showing  you how to do them whilst this is useful  the real benefit tutorials is be able to  discuss the subjects that covered in the  problem sheets with your tutor so for  example I had a problem several problem  sheets on general relativity and one of  my college tutors is a world expert on a  particular pop general relativity and  just being able to discuss the ideas  with him was fantastically useful  because he understood them on such a  deep level that that in some way gets  imparted to you lastly the tutorials  tend to happen either in your academics  office or in a room that they've booked  in your college now in the humanities  because there's more scope for choice in  the courses typically you won't have a  tutorial with a tutor from your college  instead you'll have tutorials with which  other member of the department is a  specialist in the field that is covered  in that tutorial regardless of their  college affiliation that means that you  will typically have to travel to other  colleges to have the tutorials which is  actually a really nice way of seeing the  university also instead of handing in a  property which was set by the lecturers  based on material you've covered in  lectures typically you'll be handing in  an essay which is based on material you  covered in lectures but it's largely  informed by reading that you have done  and tailored to that essay specifically  now obviously this doesn't cover  everything because in some subjects you  have to do translations in other  subjects you have to do well the variety  of things aren't essays all provinces  but this is the general idea of how the  system works I hope that you found this  useful and it's cleared up a few  misconceptions that you might have had  if there are any questions you do still  have feel free to leave them in the  comment section or send me a direct  message and thank you for watching
 

